            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>Overall, your house has a lot of excess air leakage, however there are some key areas where air leakage occurs. Air leakage was observed through electrical outlets on your exterior walls, the weather stripping along the exterior doors, and around the attic access hatch. 

I recommend to outfit electrical outlets located along the exterior walls with an electrical wall plate gasket. 

I recommend replacing the weather stripping along all of your exterior doors. Using a screw in aluminum style weather stripping may help to create a better seal, as well as using an adjustable strike plate to ensure the door makes good contact with the weather stripping while closed.  

Air leakage was also observed through the bathroom exhaust fans. I recommend you replace your exhaust fans with an ENERGY STAR?????? rated model, which will have a new damper built into the new unit.

The attic access hatch had quite a bit of air leakage entering from the attic space. I recommend using a removable caulking to seal up the access hatch. 

There was a significant amount of air leakage observed coming from the drop ceiling area above the main floor bathroom. I suspect this is coming from the attic space (as the second floor does not fully overlap the main floor, these areas that do not overlap are additional attic spaces). I suspect there is an open connection that is not sealing this vented attic space to the interior of the home. During you renos and upgrades, I highly recommend the contractor gain access into these 'side' attic spaces to ensure it is sealed from air freely entering the home. 

The header spacing along the mechnical room had quite a bit of observable air leakage. As these are already exposed, using sprayfoam to fill these cavities would likely be the easier method of insulating and air sealing. 



Savings: For a minimum 30% improvement you could potentially save $ 850 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 34.9 GJ, and qualify for up to$ 1,000 in rebates from the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program.



To be eligible for an air sealing rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, exterior main wall insulation is required. You must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>The above foundation exterior walls of your house appear to be 2x6 with R20 batt insulation, as well as 1 inch of XPS (R5). I recommend you add a minimum R10 insulation to these exterior walls, in conjunction with a future planned upgrade to your exterior siding.



The Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) that was launched on April 1, 2019 can help you cover the cost of the additional insulation. There are 3 tiers of rebates associated with how much insulation is added to the above foundation exterior walls ($3,000 for adding R10 - R14, $5,000 for adding R15 - R19, and $8,000 for adding R20+).

Savings: By adding a minimum R10 to all of the above foundation exterior walls, you could potentially save $ 130 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 5.7 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 3,000 in rebates from the DHERP.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</mainWalls>
                <ceilings>The attic space of your home appears to have an average (estimated based on what was visible from the hatch access) of R12 worth of insulation, comprised of the original fiberglass batt insulation and some additional blown fiberglass insulation on top. It appears the sloped portion of the ceiling has very little insulation, likely averaging around R8 in these areas, while selective spots along the very top (flat area) has about R40. The main floor ceiling areas were not accessible, however I highly suggest your contractor gain access into these areas, and confirm the insulation above these areas. 



I have modeled the energy savings if you were to increase the attic insulation up to a minimum R40. Please be sure you maintain adequate attic ventilation and do not block any soffit venting. Due to the space limitations in the upstairs attic, our options are fairly limited. Either determining a method to add more fiberglass batt insulation without compromising ventilation... or perhaps considering sprayfoam, which would involve romoving the interior drywall to gain access. 

I suggest discussing with your contractor for options, while AEA is happy to give advice or sggestions along the way. 



Savings: By increasing all of the attic insulation from R12 up to a minimum R40, this is estimated to save $ 340 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 14.3 GJ, and qualify for a $ 1,000 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP), if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</ceilings>
                <foundation>The basement foundation walls appear to be 8 inches of concrete with 2x4 R12. 



Your basement pony walls appear to be 2x6 framing with 6 inches of fiberglass batt, as well as 2x4 framing with 4 inches (R12) of fiberglass batt insulation. In conjuction with the main wall upgrade reommendation above, I have modelled continuing the additional R10 to the pony walls of the basement. 



Savings: By adding 2 inches (R10) rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to the pony walls, you could potentially save $ 50 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.1 GJ, and qualify for up to $ 610 rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) if you complete an eligible above foundation wall insulation upgrade.



To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior above foundation wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

</foundation>
                <HotWaterSystem>You currently have a 151 litre propane-fired hot water tank with a rated energy factor of 0.62. You mentioned you may be considering an upgrade to an on-demand model. I have modeled an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.92, could potentially save $ 210 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 9.2 GJ,  and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>Cumbustion Air Supply Pipe

Your heating and hot water appliances require air to be mixed with fuel to burn and heat your house. Without combustion air, your heating system is using the house air for combustion which may not be adequate for an efficient burn. The combustion air duct installed in your heating system room is open and freely drops cold air into the room all year long whether its needed or not. 



I recommend you install an electric damper on the combustion air duct and tie it to the operation of your heating system and water heater. This will ensure fresh air will only be introduced into the room when the heating system or water heater is operating. 



Savings: By installing an electric damper on the combustion air duct, you could potentially save $ 140 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.3 GJ . There are no rebates for installing a damper on the combustion air duct, however it will increase your homes airtightness. 

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Pellet Stove

I have modeled the estimated energy savings if you were to install a pellet stove, used for about 50% of the heating requirement for the upper floors of the house. Getting that pellet heat down into the basement is something your heating contractor can work on by using your current high efficiency furnace fan to circulate the pellet heat throughout the house.



I modeled an upgrade to a new EPA qualified pellet stove that has a dedicated outside combustion air pipe and an efficiency of at least 80%. Pellet stoves must meet either the Canadian Standards Association (CSA) B415.1-M92 (performance testing of solid-fuel burning appliances) or the U.S. Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) wood burning appliances standards (40 CFR Part 60).



Savings: By using an 80% efficient wood stove for 50% of the heating requirements of the main living area of your house, you could potentially save $ 350 per year in energy costs. Installing an eligible pellet stove qualifies for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price, up to $ 1,000 from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program

To qualify for a Deep Home Energy Retrofit Program (DHERP) rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 1.76 (R 10.0). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. To be eligible for a rebate through the DHERP, you must have both a pre-retrofit and a post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluation completed on your home by the AEA.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/deep-home-energy-retrofit-program

The upgrade recommendations outlined within this report are estimated to qualify you for $6,110 of rebates from this program. The following are the estimated rebates per component of the DHERP that the above value is comprised of.



     - $3,000 for the wall insulation

     - $500 for the attic insulation

     - $610 for the foundation pony wall insulation

     - $1,000 for the air leakage reduction

     - $1,000 for the bonus rebate



In addition to the rebates from the DHERP, you are also eligible for other rebates from Energy Efficiency Rebate Program (http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program), including the following items that may be of interest to you for this house:



     - EPA qualified pellet stove - 50% of the appliance purchase cost, up to $1,000

     - ESTAR Instantaneous, on-demand, hot water heater, with an energy factor of 0.92 or greater - $750

     - ESTAR fridge &amp; washing machine - $200 each

     - ESTAR ventless (condensing) clothes dryer - $400

     - ECM Pumps (the water circulation pump) - $175 each - 2 per fiscal year

     - Programmable Thermostats - 25% of unit cost, up to $175 each - 2 per fiscal year

     - LED Lighting - see next section.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



LED Lights ????????? Hydro Community

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to fluorescent lighting. Fluorescent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year) from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



ECM PUMPS

Electronically Commutated Motors (ECM) save pumping energy for circulation pumps and hydronic heating systems, such as baseboard convectors and in floor radiant by at least 50% and usually much more compared with other pumps. Typical pumps use a constant amount of power regardless of how much water needs to be circulated throughout the building, and are often significantly oversized. By contrast an ECM pump senses how hard it has to work to circulate the heating fluid and adjusts the flow rate accordingly. In addition, by reducing the flow, they can also increase the temperature difference between water leaving the boiler and the water returning which also saves heating fuel by increasing boiler efficiency.  These pumps determine the minimal output required to achieve optimal comfort. 



Some ECM circulators use an integrated logic board to ?????????learn????????? the system?????????s usage patterns in order to boost the standard 50 percent energy savings into the 70-80 percent range. As the valves in the different sections open or close with rising or falling demand, the pumps automatically ramp up or down to meet the load. Even though these pumps are typically 2-3 times more expensive, with the huge savings potential, their payback is typically very quick.  When you purchase and install an ECM pump in your house (up to 2 per fiscal year) you can qualify for $ 175 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



FUEL COSTS

The following values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.04 min, $0.33 per kWh

Oil  - $0.91 per litre 

Propane - $0.61 per litre

Wood - $420 per cord (purchased locally)

Pellets - $5.67 per bag

